{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/518799-stories-of-the-embrace-or-secret-origins","id":518799,"title":"Stories of the Embrace, or Secret Origins","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-18T20:33:28-07:00","id":518799,"title":"Stories of the Embrace, or Secret Origins","content":"The Embrace is obviously a very big part of the game. I could just be imagining it but it seems like in most games you do start off already Embraced, consigning the act to part of your background that may or may not be touched upon in-game.<br /> <br /> So I was wondering how your PCs or NPCs got Embraced, or how you imagine certain vampires Embracing others. Obviously with revenants and the unintentional Embrace we have a lot more potential tales of how it happened.<br /> <br /> Do you imagine most vampires prepare their childe before the Embrace? Do they take time out of their nightlife afterwards to show them the ropes? Or do they do it on a whim and leave a trail of abandoned childer behind them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-19T00:08:17-07:00","id":519035,"title":"","content":"(My experience comes from a V20 game, but it could easily have been Requiem 2e with no modification except clan names.)<br /> <br /> Our ST wanted us to experience becoming Vampires rather than start that way. Hugely influenced by The Blue Book, he had a lot of WoD &quot;Mortals&quot; kind of game before. This was his first foray into Vampires in any &quot;playable&quot; depth and he wanted it to be bathed in horror and ambiance rather than just a &quot;power up&quot;.<br /> <br /> ***<br /> <br /> Until now, we had three &quot;time periods&quot; separated by 15 years time skips.<br /> <br /> Our group started as 10 years old kids investigating strange murders happening in our neighborhood. This was our first experience of the supernatural, an adventure of improvised occult detective kids chasing shadows and (unbeknown to us at the time) vampires, at night and after school. It's a LOT more amazing than the description lets out, playing kids was really an tremendous experience of vulnerability and frailty, as well as resourcefulness.<br /> <br /> This had the immediate advantage of cementing what would become a group of friends later in life.<br /> <br /> Insert 15 years time skip once our supernatural quest was solved.<br /> <br /> We are now 25 years old, and we have moved on in our lives, becoming adults while keeping in touch with old friends. Some of us had become rich or famous (coming from privileged families). Strange events coincide and we learn that old enemies are coming back. We are still mortals. Our group became divided, some of us almost joining vampire hunters. But my character was <i>fascinated </i>by vampires, but knew very little, and his first encounter with one as a child had spawned an obsession. He started chasing a Sabbat vampire around, until he was forced to subdue him. After some questionning, he decided the &quot;Camarilla&quot; seemed <i>a lot nicer</i>, so he set out to try and find them. (I'm cutting out a lot of details.) It was largely my character's obsession with Vampires that led my group down the rabbit hole, for they would have become hunters most likely if I hadn't so often interceded in favor of the vamps.<br /> <br /> My plan was to trade my character's fortune -- around 200 million dollars -- for the right to be sired by a (low generation) vampire (typical VTM meta stuff, heh), which, in the context of the game made sense since we knew the five Primogen members far more than their subalterns. The first time,<i> I was Dominated by the Tremere into believing I had been Embraced</i>, so she could get all my money and not waste time with a Childe. I was saved by a lawyer friend who immediately saw I was acting weird. It was a hilarious moment, and it made perfect sense for the Elder to try and trick me, especially since I was obviously one of those eccentric and naive millionaires one could easily rob.<br /> <br /> The second time I made the same offer, it was with the Toreador Elder, called The White Lady. She asked me to <i>abduct a particular person of her taste</i>, in addition to my fortune, a person that she had always wanted to Embrace but couldn't get her to come willingly. *TRIGGER WARNING* As a test, I was thus forced to comply and kidnap my childhood friend... asking my own security guards to use tasers and tie her... a very unsavory moment... we were both Embraced in the same room... she was tied to a bed... I'm sparing you all the details. This whole scene was basically an upfront version of what vampires actually do a lot (<a href=\"http://www.flurtmag.com/2012/09/vampires-rape-culture/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.flurtmag.com/2012/09/vampires-rape-culture/</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/263295-doing-heinous-shit-to-your-players-where-does-the-line-go\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...es-the-line-go</a>). When I woke up, humans treated like bloodbags were shown to us. Overwhelmed with guilt about being complicit of what happened, and in front of people who were obviously deprived of their free will, I drank. This, and the immediately preceding events got my character thinking he may have been foolhardy about so quickly asking to become and join the Vampires. His Requiem started guilt-wracked about indirectly forcing the Embrace on his friend. She was even forced to drink blood by her Sire. (She also had a grievous light intolerance that included moonlight, a rare mutation, but was also resistant to blood bounds.)<br /> <br /> My Requiem thus started by depriving humans of their consent, first by being complicit in the abduction and the rest... and thereafter by realizing I had become become a parasite and predator to humans. My Sire, being truly obsessed by the young women she asked me to kidnap, sent me on &quot;errands&quot; more than once to get her back when she tried to escape her captor.<br /> <br /> We've had another time skip since then. And <i>a lot</i> more has gone on, none of it as dark as my Embrace (*shudders*). Last game, one of the characters, now a 40 years old Priest, finally put an end to my despicable Sire (though my character is extremely resentful of that, given the blood bond in place). Being blood-bounded to someone who commits heinous acts of evil and directs you to do the same is not exactly what my initially human character had foreseen for his Requiem. At least my character may now find a bit of respite...<br /> <br /> ***<br /> <br /> As you can see, some pretty dark themes were brought up around the Embrace. The ST wanted us to feel the horror of Vampires, so we wouldn't feel as though we were &quot;Cursed with Awesome&quot;, or that we were just another kind of &quot;supers&quot; or just superheroes with harmless &quot;edge and grit&quot;. The price was always paid in horror <i>dearly</i>, perhaps more than in an average game. While we had to discuss sensitive issues, its definitely <i>not </i>for every group (and we certainly could have handled it better too).<br /> <br /> But the result is that, strangely, experiencing the disgusting aspects of being a vampire made the experience a lot more real for me, more than I have felt for many characters in the past. I feel &quot;real&quot; guilt for my character's choices, in a sense. That character is no longer able to simply accept killing (you know, in the RPG &quot;combat&quot; sense), and has become obsessed about proving to himself and others that he can be good... but he slips every now and then...<br /> <br /> I feel going from mortal to Vampire during play has strengthened the bond we have with our characters, and made us realize we were playing <u><b>monsters</b></u>.<br /> <br /> <b>Difficulties with in-game Embrace</b> (that we experienced somehow)<ul><li>Knowing about the existence of Vampires in the first place, and knowing how you can become one (can be helped with an occultist friend or good plot hooks...)</li> <li>Dealing with the whole &quot;why should I want that&quot; and the &quot;Cursed with Awesome&quot; trope/metagame problem; meaning you can't really complain about immortality and power if you don't pay a dire price; why would a character want that or <i>not want that</i>?</li> <li>Finding an actual prospective Sire despite the Masquerade, and survive the encounter to tell the tale!</li> <li>Finding a Sire of the <i>appropriate </i>clan, despite the choice being very metagame at this point (player vs. character knowledge in full swing here). It's hard to justify refusing a vampire Sire of the &quot;wrong&quot; clan if you don't know enough about &quot;clans&quot; in the first place... but since you shouldn't know, masquerade-oblige... conundrum...</li> <li>Justifying to the prospective Sire why the character is a good &quot;Vampire candidate&quot;, and dealing with the fact he's doing you a favor, how do you pay it back?</li> <li>Making sure you don't just get killed in the process, especially if the exchange of goods or favors is already done. It's death we're dealing with here!</li> <li>Alternatively: the ST could simply make the choice for you and design an encounter where all those questions are dealt with by having the Sire pick you without you having a say... (hopefully this is preceded by a talk with the ST and an agreement of some sort)</li> <li>Years of servitude before being freed from Sire, depending on local tradition and circumstances...</li> </ul><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5602,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5602-barbechenue","user":"BarbeChenue","date":". 05-19-2015, 03:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5602-barbechenue","name":"BarbeChenue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5602_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"80"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-19T11:15:50-07:00","id":519803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BarbeChenue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/518799-stories-of-the-embrace-or-secret-origins?p=519035#post519035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>Alternatively: the ST could simply make the choice for you and design an encounter where all those questions are dealt with by having the Sire pick you without you having a say... (hopefully this is preceded by a talk with the ST and an agreement of some sort)</li> <li>Years of servitude before being freed from Sire, depending on local tradition and circumstances...</li> </ul><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think being chosen by your Sire is somewhat the default assumption in Vampire. Either you were bloodbonded or a Ghoul first and Embraced after a good time of servitude, or you were picked out because you had the ideal characteristics a vampire is looking for in a Childe. This also makes it far easier for the player, assuming there is OOC agreement of what kind of Vampire does the Embrace.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BarbeChenue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/518799-stories-of-the-embrace-or-secret-origins?p=519035#post519035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>Dealing with the whole &quot;why should I want that&quot; and the &quot;Cursed with Awesome&quot; trope/metagame problem; meaning you can't really complain about immortality and power if you don't pay a dire price; why would a character want that or <i>not want that</i>?</li> </ul><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think as a Vampire you do pay a dire price. A lot of people want to have children, which becomes impossible. Also, daysleep causes a lot of problems and you can't really continue your career or keep your earlier life because if this. You will probably also lose most of your friends, because of scheduling problems and the fact that you will now have to hunt or spend time on Vampire politics, becoming part of the All Night Society. Growing old with your loved ones also becomes impossible (unless you do it in a fucked up Vampire way).<br /> <br /> I could probably go on for a while. Maybe that teenagers would think that being a Vampire would be cool and such, because they don't think about most of the drawbacks, but if you are an adult in a nice and stable life situation, becoming a Vampire will really wreck that and your life goals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/304-penta","name":"Penta","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar304_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"617"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}